/**
 * check-font-coverage.mjs - can the badge draw every character it ships?
 *
 * WHY THIS CHECK EXISTS
 * ---------------------
 * The badge ships four LVGL faces generated by lv_font_conv from a SUBSET of Noto
 * Sans SC Medium (main/ui/fonts, see the licence file next to them). A subset font
 * holds only the glyphs its --symbols list named, so a string using a character
 * outside that list renders as a blank box: a silent, per-string failure no host
 * build can see and that is easy to miss on the device.
 *
 * Coverage is a property of the specific strings we ship and of the specific face
 * each one is drawn in, so it has to be measured against them. That is what this
 * file does.
 *
 *
 * THE THREE HARD CLASSES (a miss in any of them fails the run)
 * -----------------------------------------------------------
 * 1. PER-ELEMENT COVERAGE -- the check that matters.
 *    For every element in badge_els[]: take the code points reachable in the string
 *    it draws -- the element's `text` template AND, for every "{token}" that
 *    template contains, the code points of every value that can substitute into
 *    that token, recursively (badge_ui_expand() re-expands substituted values, and
 *    the specification's places are stored as "{name}在公司楼下花坛") -- and require
 *    every one of them to exist in THAT ELEMENT'S OWN face
 *    (badge_fonts[el.font]). A face is not a single font: the 24 px clock face
 *    having no glyph for a character someone just put in the clock is exactly the
 *    defect a union check cannot express.
 *    Part 1b is the same idea for the strings that no single element owns but the
 *    badge still draws or says (the alert wording, a care's refusal text, the
 *    texts.* pool, the voice words), measured against the faces the specification's
 *    own composite components declare (BADGE_BUBBLE_FONT / BADGE_TOAST_FONT /
 *    BADGE_FLOAT_FONT).
 * 2. FIRMWARE LITERALS THAT REACH THE GLASS.
 *    Every non-ASCII literal in the sources that build on-glass text must be
 *    covered by at least one face. See GLASS_SOURCES / NOT_GLASS_SOURCES below:
 *    the split between them is the whole reason this check is trustworthy, so both
 *    lists are written out rather than derived.
 * 3. THE SYMBOL LIST ITSELF.
 *    chars_ui.txt is the specification package's own symbol list. Every code point
 *    in it must be covered by the faces that DECLARE they implement it
 *    (badge_spec.json fonts.*.chars == "ui"). This is the check that stays
 *    meaningful if someone regenerates a font.
 *
 *
 * INFORMATIONAL (printed, counted, and deliberately NOT a failure)
 * ---------------------------------------------------------------
 * A clean run does NOT mean "everything in the package has a glyph", and the output
 * says so. Three groups are reported with their origin and do not fail:
 *
 *   a. specification PROSE -- .screen.doc, .modes.*.doc, .quirks[*].doc, .hw.rows,
 *      .fontFile, ... The badge never draws any of it; the old version of this
 *      check walked the whole badge_spec.json and reported these as missing glyphs,
 *      which is how a green build was turned red by a character in a paragraph of
 *      documentation.
 *   b. the 14 upstream-event descriptions (events[i][3] and events[i][4], e.g.
 *      "命令词编号", "新值", and the "—" placeholder). They document the two numbers
 *      in the 8-byte uplink record for the App's decoder; no badge screen draws
 *      them.
 *   c. the App/radio-facing sources in NOT_GLASS_SOURCES -- text the phone renders
 *      (net_proto.c's social handshake, pet_state.c's item and breed names).
 *
 * --strict promotes all three groups to hard failures, for when someone wants the
 * whole package covered. It is expected to fail today, and the reason is printed
 * with it.
 *
 *
 * WHAT THIS FILE DELIBERATELY DOES NOT DO
 * ---------------------------------------
 *  * It does not walk badge_spec.json as a whole. Almost none of that JSON is text
 *    the badge draws, and a check that fails on prose is a check somebody deletes.
 *  * It does not check C comments. Comments never reach the glass; the vocabulary
 *    they copy (badge_spec.h's element and event enums, badge_fsm.h's mode names)
 *    is already covered through the tables that are actually drawn.
 *  * It does not treat main/ui/badge_spec.h as a glass source. That header is
 *    FROZEN and its Chinese is documentation.
 *
 *
 * USAGE
 * -----
 *   node tools/check-font-coverage.mjs [--strict] [--all]
 *                                      [--spec <path>] [--fonts-dir <dir>]
 *                                      [--chars <path>]
 *   --strict  informational misses become failures
 *   --all     list every informational code point, not just the first few
 *
 * Exit codes: 0 = clean, 1 = a class-1/2/3 miss (or any miss under --strict),
 *             2 = the checker could not trust its input (see `bail`).
 */

import { existsSync, readFileSync, readdirSync, statSync } from 'node:fs';
import { dirname, extname, join, resolve } from 'node:path';
import { fileURLToPath } from 'node:url';

const HERE = dirname(fileURLToPath(import.meta.url));
const ROOT = resolve(HERE, '..');

/* ------------------------------------------------------------------- args ----*/

const argv = process.argv.slice(2);
const argOf = (name, dflt) => {
  const i = argv.indexOf(name);
  return i >= 0 && argv[i + 1] ? argv[i + 1] : dflt;
};
const STRICT = argv.includes('--strict');
const LIST_ALL = argv.includes('--all');

if (argv.includes('--help') || argv.includes('-h')) {
  console.log(
    'usage: node tools/check-font-coverage.mjs [--strict] [--all]\n' +
      '                                        [--spec <path>] [--fonts-dir <dir>] [--chars <path>]\n' +
      '  --strict  promote the informational classes (spec prose, the uplink-event\n' +
      '            descriptions, the App/radio-facing and legacy sources) to failures\n' +
      '  --all     list every informational code point, not just the first few',
  );
  process.exit(0);
}

const SPEC_PATH = resolve(
  argOf('--spec', join(ROOT, '_ref', 'baji-spec', '吧唧交互规范', 'badge_spec.json')),
);
const SPEC_DIR = dirname(SPEC_PATH);
const FONTS_DIR = resolve(argOf('--fonts-dir', join(ROOT, 'main', 'ui', 'fonts')));
const CHARS_PATH = resolve(argOf('--chars', join(SPEC_DIR, 'fonts', 'chars_ui.txt')));
const SPEC_DATA_C = join(ROOT, 'main', 'ui', 'badge_spec_data.c');
const SPEC_DATA_H = join(ROOT, 'main', 'ui', 'badge_spec_data.h');

const MAX_LISTED = 24;

/* ------------------------------------------------------------------ output ---*/

let hardMisses = 0;
let softMisses = 0;

const u = (cp) => `U+${cp.toString(16).toUpperCase().padStart(4, '0')}`;
const esc = (cp) =>
  cp > 0xffff
    ? `\\U${cp.toString(16).padStart(8, '0')}`
    : `\\u${cp.toString(16).padStart(4, '0')}`;
const clip = (s, n = 52) => {
  const c = [...s];
  return c.length > n ? `${c.slice(0, n).join('')}...` : s;
};
const nonAscii = (s) => [...s].filter((ch) => ch.codePointAt(0) > 0x7f);
const title = (s) => console.log(`\n${s}`);

/**
 * The input cannot be trusted. This is a different failure from a missing glyph:
 * it means the checker would otherwise be reporting a happy number it cannot stand
 * behind, so it stops with a distinct exit code instead.
 */
const bail = (msg) => {
  console.error(`check-font-coverage: ERROR: ${msg}`);
  process.exit(2);
};

const failer = () => (STRICT ? 'HARD (--strict)' : 'hard');

/* ================================================================== part I ====*/
/* Lexing C, so that comments and string literals can never be confused.        */
/* ====================================================================== ======*/

/**
 * Split C source into `code` (comments and string/char literal bodies blanked out,
 * line structure preserved so offsets still map to line numbers) and the list of
 * string literals with their line numbers.
 *
 * A regex over the raw text cannot do this correctly: a `"` inside a comment (this
 * repo has them) unbalances the scan, and a `/*` inside a string (a URL) starts a
 * "comment" that eats the rest of the file.
 */
function lexC(src) {
  const strings = [];
  let code = '';
  let i = 0;
  const n = src.length;  while (i < n) {
    const c = src[i];
    if (c === '/' && src[i + 1] === '*') {
      const e = src.indexOf('*/', i + 2);
      const stop = e < 0 ? n : e + 2;
      code += src.slice(i, stop).replace(/[^\n]/g, ' ');
      i = stop;
      continue;
    }
    if (c === '/' && src[i + 1] === '/') {
      const e = src.indexOf('\n', i);
      const stop = e < 0 ? n : e;
      code += ' '.repeat(stop - i);
      i = stop;
      continue;
    }
    if (c === '"') {
      let j = i + 1;
      let body = '';
      while (j < n && src[j] !== '"') {
        if (src[j] === '\\') {
          body += src[j] + (src[j + 1] ?? '');
          j += 2;
          continue;
        }
        body += src[j];
        j++;
      }
      const line = src.slice(0, i).split('\n').length;
      /* The literal's IDENTITY is kept in `code` (as the decoded text, escapes
       * resolved), not blanked out: the generated tables this check reads are made of
       * string literals, and the tables' row boundaries are the only thing that must
       * not be confused with a comma inside a string. Comments and surrounded layout
       * still cannot leak in, because they are blanked before this point. */
      strings.push({ body, line });
      code += `"${unescapeC(body).replace(/[\\"]/g, ' ')}"`;
      i = j + 1;
      continue;
    }
    if (c === "'") {
      const m = /^'(?:\\.|[^'\\])'/.exec(src.slice(i, i + 10));
      if (m) {
        code += ' '.repeat(m[0].length);
        i += m[0].length;
        continue;
      }
    }
    code += c;
    i++;
  }
  return { code, strings };
}

/** A C string literal body as the characters the badge would draw. */
function unescapeC(body) {
  let out = '';
  for (let i = 0; i < body.length; i++) {
    if (body[i] !== '\\') {
      out += body[i];
      continue;
    }
    const e = body[i + 1];
    if (e === 'u' || e === 'U') {
      const len = e === 'u' ? 4 : 8;
      const hex = body.slice(i + 2, i + 2 + len);
      if (/^[0-9a-fA-F]+$/.test(hex)) {
        out += String.fromCodePoint(parseInt(hex, 16));
        i += 1 + len;
        continue;
      }
    }
    if (e === 'x') {
      const hex = /^[0-9a-fA-F]+/.exec(body.slice(i + 2));
      if (hex) {
        out += String.fromCodePoint(parseInt(hex[0], 16));
        i += 1 + hex[0].length;
        continue;
      }
    }
    const simple = { n: '\n', t: '\t', r: '\r', 0: '\0', '\\': '\\', '"': '"', "'": "'" }[e];
    out += simple !== undefined ? simple : e ?? '';
    i++;
  }
  return out;
}

/** The `{ ... }` body of `name[] = { ... }` / `name[COUNT] = { ... }`. */
function arrayBody(code, name) {
  const re = new RegExp(`\\b${name}\\s*\\[\\s*\\w*\\s*\\]\\s*=\\s*\\{`);
  const m = re.exec(code);
  if (!m) return null;
  const start = code.indexOf('{', m.index);
  let depth = 0;
  for (let i = start; i < code.length; i++) {
    if (code[i] === '{') depth++;
    else if (code[i] === '}') {
      depth--;
      if (depth === 0) return { body: code.slice(start + 1, i), start: start + 1 };
    }
  }
  return null;
}

/** Top-level `{ ... }` rows of an initialiser body, with their start offsets. */
function braceRows(body, base = 0) {
  const rows = [];
  let depth = 0;
  let start = -1;
  for (let i = 0; i < body.length; i++) {
    if (body[i] === '{') {
      if (depth === 0) start = i + 1;
      depth++;
    } else if (body[i] === '}') {
      depth--;
      if (depth === 0) rows.push({ text: body.slice(start, i), at: base + start });
    }
  }
  return rows;
}

/** The comma-separated fields of one row, at its own brace depth. */
function rowFields(row) {
  const out = [];
  let depth = 0;
  let cur = '';
  for (const ch of row) {
    if (ch === '{' || ch === '(') depth++;
    else if (ch === '}' || ch === ')') depth--;
    if (ch === ',' && depth === 0) {
      out.push(cur.trim());
      cur = '';
      continue;
    }
    cur += ch;
  }
  if (cur.trim().length) out.push(cur.trim());
  return out;
}

const lineAt = (code, offset) => {
  let line = 1;
  for (let i = 0; i < offset && i < code.length; i++) if (code[i] === '\n') line++;
  return line;
};

/* ================================================================= part II ====*/
/* The fonts' cmaps, parsed the way LVGL resolves them.                         */
/* ====================================================================== ======*/

/**
 * `coveredCodePoints(fontPath)` -- every code point a generated LVGL font can draw.
 *
 * The file is C, but its shape is fixed by lv_font_conv, so the parse is a real
 * `lv_font_fmt_txt_cmap_t` parse: the data arrays are collected, then `cmaps[]` is
 * resolved as LVGL's get_glyph_dsc_id() does it
 * (managed_components/lvgl__lvgl/src/font/fmt_txt/lv_font_fmt_txt.c):
 *
 *   FORMAT0_*        a contiguous range, code point = range_start + i
 *   SPARSE_*         unicode_list holds offsets from range_start, sorted
 *   *_FULL           glyph_id_ofs_list can mark an individual code point missing
 *                    (offset 0 anywhere but at range_start means "no glyph")
 *   *_TINY           no ofs list, every code point in the range has a glyph
 *
 * `list_length` is the length of both lists, so a disagreement with the array that
 * was actually parsed is itself a parse failure rather than a silent short count.
 *
 * As a second, independent reading of the same file, the generator's own per-glyph
 * comments (`/* U+4E2D "中" *​/`) are collected and the two sets are required to
 * agree. If they ever do not, this parse cannot be trusted and the checker stops
 * rather than reporting a number it cannot stand behind. (lv_font_conv's 4-byte
 * packed format still spells every code point that way; "origin" is written
 * separately for exactly this reason.)
 */
function coveredCodePoints(fontPath) {
  const text = readFileSync(fontPath, 'utf8');

  const arrays = new Map();
  const arrayRe =
    /static\s+(?:LV_ATTRIBUTE_LARGE_CONST\s+)?const\s+(?:uint8_t|uint16_t|int16_t|int8_t)\s+(\w+)\[\]\s*=\s*\{([\s\S]*?)\n\};/g;
  for (const m of text.matchAll(arrayRe)) {
    const body = m[2].replace(/\/\*[\s\S]*?\*\//g, ' ');
    arrays.set(
      m[1],
      body
        .split(',')
        .map((s) => s.trim())
        .filter((s) => s.length > 0)
        .map((s) => {
          if (!/^-?(?:0[xX][0-9a-fA-F]+|\d+)$/.test(s)) {
            bail(`${fontPath}: non-numeric entry "${s}" in ${m[1]}[]`);
          }
          return Number(s);
        }),
    );
  }

  const cm = /cmaps\[\]\s*=\s*\{([\s\S]*?)\n\};/.exec(text);
  if (!cm) bail(`${fontPath}: no "cmaps[]" table -- is this an LVGL font file?`);

  const declared = /\.cmap_num\s*=\s*(\d+)/.exec(text);
  const covered = new Set();
  let segments = 0;

  for (const m of cm[1].matchAll(/\{([^{}]*)\}/g)) {
    const body = m[1];
    const field = (name) => {
      const f = new RegExp(`\\.${name}\\s*=\\s*([^,\\n]+)`).exec(body);
      return f ? f[1].trim() : null;
    };
    const rangeStart = Number(field('range_start'));
    const rangeLength = Number(field('range_length'));
    const type = field('type');
    const listName = field('unicode_list');
    const ofsName = field('glyph_id_ofs_list');
    const listLength = Number(field('list_length'));
    if (!Number.isFinite(rangeStart) || !Number.isFinite(rangeLength) || !type) {
      bail(`${fontPath}: unparseable cmaps[] entry: ${body.replace(/\s+/g, ' ').trim()}`);
    }
    segments++;

    const ofs = ofsName && ofsName !== 'NULL' ? arrays.get(ofsName) : null;
    if (ofsName && ofsName !== 'NULL' && !ofs) {
      bail(`${fontPath}: cmaps[] references unknown glyph_id_ofs_list ${ofsName}`);
    }
    if (ofs && ofs.length !== listLength) {
      bail(`${fontPath}: ${ofsName} holds ${ofs.length} entries; list_length says ${listLength}`);
    }
    /* The first character of the range is always valid; a 0 anywhere else in a
     * *_FULL list means "no glyph here". */
    const hasGlyph = (i, cp) => !(ofs && ofs[i] === 0 && cp !== rangeStart);

    if (/_SPARSE_/.test(type)) {
      const list = listName && listName !== 'NULL' ? arrays.get(listName) : null;
      if (!list) bail(`${fontPath}: cmaps[] references unknown unicode_list ${listName}`);
      if (list.length !== listLength) {
        bail(`${fontPath}: ${listName} holds ${list.length} entries; list_length says ${listLength}`);
      }
      list.forEach((offset, i) => {
        const cp = rangeStart + offset;
        if (hasGlyph(i, cp)) covered.add(cp);
      });
    } else if (/_FORMAT0_/.test(type)) {
      if (listLength !== 0) {
        bail(`${fontPath}: a FORMAT0 cmap declares list_length ${listLength}`);
      }
      for (let i = 0; i < rangeLength; i++) {
        const cp = rangeStart + i;
        if (hasGlyph(i, cp)) covered.add(cp);
      }
    } else {
      bail(`${fontPath}: unknown cmap type ${type}`);
    }
  }

  if (declared && Number(declared[1]) !== segments) {
    bail(
      `${fontPath}: .cmap_num = ${declared[1]} but cmaps[] holds ${segments} entries -- ` +
        'the parse would miss a segment',
    );
  }
  if (covered.size === 0) bail(`${fontPath}: the cmap covers no code points`);

  /* The second reading: the generator's own comment per glyph. */
  const commented = new Set();
  for (const m of text.matchAll(/U\+([0-9A-Fa-f]{4,6})\b/g)) commented.add(parseInt(m[1], 16));
  if (commented.size > 0) {
    const onlyCmap = [...covered].filter((cp) => !commented.has(cp));
    const onlyComment = [...commented].filter((cp) => !covered.has(cp));
    if (onlyCmap.length || onlyComment.length) {
      const fmt = (l) => l.slice(0, 8).map((cp) => `${u(cp)} '${String.fromCodePoint(cp)}'`).join(', ');
      bail(
        `${fontPath}: the cmap parse disagrees with the file's own "U+XXXX" glyph comments\n` +
          `  in the cmap but not in a comment (${onlyCmap.length}): ${fmt(onlyCmap)}\n` +
          `  in a comment but not in the cmap (${onlyComment.length}): ${fmt(onlyComment)}`,
      );
    }
  }

  return { covered, segments };
}

/* ================================================================ part III ====*/
/* The generated tables (badge_spec_data.c), the only glass vocabulary.         */
/* ====================================================================== ======*/

const specSrc = existsSync(SPEC_DATA_C)
  ? lexC(readFileSync(SPEC_DATA_C, 'utf8'))
  : bail(`${SPEC_DATA_C} is missing -- run: node tools/gen-badge-ui.mjs`);
const specHdr = existsSync(SPEC_DATA_H)
  ? lexC(readFileSync(SPEC_DATA_H, 'utf8'))
  : bail(`${SPEC_DATA_H} is missing -- run: node tools/gen-badge-ui.mjs`);

const tableBody = (name) => {
  const b = arrayBody(specSrc.code, name);
  if (!b) bail(`${SPEC_DATA_C}: no ${name}[] table -- is it the generator's output?`);
  return b;
};
const tableRows = (name) => {
  const b = tableBody(name);
  return braceRows(b.body, b.start);
};

/** A flat table of string literals (`const char *const x[] = { "a", "b" };`). */
const tableStrings = (name) => {
  const b = tableBody(name);
  return [...b.body.matchAll(/"((?:[^"\\]|\\.)*)"/g)].map((m) => unescapeC(m[1]));
};

/* The string pool. Index 0 is the empty string ("none"), so an index read out of a
 * table is used directly as an array index, exactly as the firmware does. */
const strings = (() => {
  const out = tableStrings('badge_strings');
  const declaredH = /#define\s+BADGE_STRING_COUNT\s+(\d+)/.exec(specHdr.code);
  if (!declaredH) bail(`${SPEC_DATA_H}: no BADGE_STRING_COUNT`);
  if (out.length !== Number(declaredH[1])) {
    bail(
      `${SPEC_DATA_C}: parsed ${out.length} strings but BADGE_STRING_COUNT says ${declaredH[1]}`,
    );
  }
  return out;
})();

const strAt = (idx) => {
  const i = Number(idx);
  if (!Number.isFinite(i)) return '';
  return strings[i] ?? '';
};

const defineIndex = (defineName) => {
  const m = new RegExp(`#define\\s+${defineName}\\s+(\\d+)`).exec(specHdr.code);
  return m ? Number(m[1]) : null;
};
const identToken = (s) => String(s).toUpperCase().replace(/[^A-Z0-9]+/g, '_').replace(/^_+|_+$/g, '');
const txt = (key) => {
  const idx = defineIndex(`BADGE_TXT_${identToken(key)}`);
  return idx === null ? null : strAt(idx);
};
const txtList = () => {
  const out = [];
  for (const m of specHdr.code.matchAll(/#define\s+BADGE_TXT_([A-Z0-9_]+)\s+(\d+)/g)) {
    out.push({ key: m[1], text: strAt(m[2]) });
  }
  return out;
};

const elements = tableRows('badge_els').map((r, index) => {
  const f = rowFields(r.text);
  if (f.length < 30) bail(`${SPEC_DATA_C}: badge_els[${index}] has ${f.length} fields, expected 30`);
  return {
    index,
    line: lineAt(specSrc.code, r.at),
    type: f[0],
    font: f[1],
    textIdx: Number(f[14]),
    idIdx: Number(f[15]),
    id: strAt(f[15]),
  };
});

const faceNameOf = (fontField) => {
  const m = /^BADGE_FONT_([A-Z0-9_]+)$/.exec(fontField);
  return m ? `badge_${m[1].toLowerCase()}` : null;
};

const careKeys = tableStrings('badge_care_keys');
const careDefs = tableRows('badge_care_defs').map((r, i) => {
  const f = rowFields(r.text);
  return {
    index: i,
    key: careKeys[i] ?? `#${i}`,
    label: strAt(f[0]),
    blockFirst: Number(f[f.length - 2]),
    blockCount: Number(f[f.length - 1]),
    line: lineAt(specSrc.code, r.at),
  };
});
const careBlocks = tableRows('badge_blocks').map((r, i) => {
  const f = rowFields(r.text);
  return { index: i, text: strAt(f[3]), line: lineAt(specSrc.code, r.at) };
});
const blocksOfCare = (key) => {
  const def = careDefs.find((c) => c.key === key);
  if (!def) return [];
  return careBlocks.slice(def.blockFirst, def.blockFirst + def.blockCount);
};

const statNames = tableStrings('badge_stat_names');
const stageNames = tableStrings('badge_stage_names');
const quirkNames = tableRows('badge_quirk_defs').map((r) => {
  const f = rowFields(r.text);
  return { name: strAt(f[1]), line: lineAt(specSrc.code, r.at) };
});
const voiceDefs = tableRows('badge_voice_defs').map((r) => {
  const f = rowFields(r.text);
  return { word: strAt(f[1]), reply: strAt(f[3]), line: lineAt(specSrc.code, r.at) };
});
const placeDefs = tableRows('badge_place_defs').map((r) => {
  const f = rowFields(r.text);
  return { text: strAt(f[1]), sub: strAt(f[2]), line: lineAt(specSrc.code, r.at) };
});
const guideDefs = tableRows('badge_guide_defs').map((r) => {
  const f = rowFields(r.text);
  return { title: strAt(f[1]), sub: strAt(f[2]), line: lineAt(specSrc.code, r.at) };
});
const alertDefs = tableRows('badge_alerts').map((r) => {
  const f = rowFields(r.text);
  return { text: strAt(f[2]), line: lineAt(specSrc.code, r.at) };
});
const touchTargets = tableRows('badge_touch_targets').map((r) => {
  const f = rowFields(r.text);
  return { label: strAt(f[0]), line: lineAt(specSrc.code, r.at) };
});

/* The faces, in badge_fonts[] order. badge_spec_data.h names them
 * (BADGE_FONT_ID_F16 = 0, ...) and the generator emits badge_fonts[] in that same
 * order, so the face an element uses is BADGE_FONT_ID_<NAME> -> main/ui/fonts/
 * badge_<name>.c. A fifth face would be picked up here instead of being silently
 * unchecked. */
const faceRows = (() => {
  const rows = braceRows(tableBody('badge_fonts').body).length;
  const ids = [...specHdr.code.matchAll(/#define\s+BADGE_FONT_ID_([A-Z0-9_]+)\s+(\d+)/g)]
    .map((m) => ({ name: `badge_${m[1].toLowerCase()}`, index: Number(m[2]) }))
    .sort((a, b) => a.index - b.index);
  if (ids.length === 0) bail(`${SPEC_DATA_H}: no BADGE_FONT_ID_* -- cannot name the faces`);
  if (ids.length !== rows) {
    bail(
      `${SPEC_DATA_C}: badge_fonts[] has ${rows} rows but ${SPEC_DATA_H} declares ` +
        `${ids.length} BADGE_FONT_ID_* faces -- the font enum and the generated table disagree`,
    );
  }
  if (ids.some((f, i) => f.index !== i)) {
    bail(`${SPEC_DATA_H}: BADGE_FONT_ID_* is not a dense 0..N-1 numbering of the faces`);
  }
  return ids.map((f) => f.name);
})();

const faces = new Map();
for (const name of faceRows) {
  const path = join(FONTS_DIR, `${name}.c`);
  if (!existsSync(path)) {
    bail(
      `${path} is missing.\n` +
        '  The four generated faces live in main/ui/fonts/ (see docs/badge-assets-integration.md).',
    );
  }
  const { covered, segments } = coveredCodePoints(path);
  faces.set(name, { name, path, covered, segments });
}
const allFaces = new Set();
for (const f of faces.values()) for (const cp of f.covered) allFaces.add(cp);
const unionCovered = (cps) => [...cps].every((cp) => allFaces.has(cp));

/* ================================================================= part IV ====*/
/* {token} -> every value that can be substituted into it.                      */
/* ====================================================================== ======*/

/*
 * The mapping is the reference renderer's own fill()
 * (_ref/baji-spec/吧唧交互规范/吧唧渲染参考.html, function fill(t)) and the token
 * contract tests/host/test_badge_geom.c pins, resolved against the GENERATED tables
 * -- nothing here is a second, hand-typed copy of a string.
 *
 *   {careLabel:<key>}  the care's label, and every refusal message of that care
 *                      (fill(): `blockOf(k) || S.care[k].label`). badge_ui_expand()
 *                      documents the same rule.
 *   {stage}            badge_stage_names[]
 *   {placeText}        badge_place_defs[].text  -- themselves templates, expanded again
 *   {placeSub}         badge_place_defs[].sub
 *   {guideTitle}       badge_guide_defs[].title
 *   {guideSub}         badge_guide_defs[].sub
 *   {phoneText} {syncText} {voiceState} {meetGift} {sleepNow}
 *                      the named entries of the texts pool (BADGE_TXT_*)
 *   {brightText}       the three brightness levels. A closed set the spec fixes
 *                      (badge_fsm.h: "0 低 / 1 中 / 2 高"; the reference's
 *                      BRIGHT=[['低',.45],['中',.75],['高',1]]), so it is measured.
 *   {ttXY} {ttText}    computed by badge_ui.c (badge_ui_plan.h's tt_xy / tt_text); the
 *                      package ships no such string, and badge_ui.c does not exist
 *                      yet, so nothing is measured against them here. The reference's
 *                      own defaults are checked separately and INFORMATIONALly --
 *                      see REFERENCE_RUNTIME_TOKENS -- because adopting that wording
 *                      is a decision, not a fact.
 *   {name} {peer} {peerBreed}
 *                      the pet's name / the peer's name / the peer's breed. The
 *                      specification ships no such string -- the App and the owner
 *                      supply them -- so the honest bound is every Chinese word this
 *                      package puts in a pet's mouth: the texts pool, the quirk
 *                      names and the voice vocabulary. It is a bound, not a proof,
 *                      and it is written down here as one.
 *   {time} {bat} {coins} {growth} {nextGrowth} {step} {n} {ver} {pairCode}
 *   {food} {mood} {clean} {energy} {health}
 *                      numbers, a clock and a version: the runtime renders ASCII
 *                      digits, ':' and '.', so the template's own characters are the
 *                      whole contribution.
 *   {placeIcon} {guideIcon}
 *                      icon names, not text.
 */
const textsPool = [...new Set(txtList().map((t) => t.text).filter(Boolean))];
const namePool = [
  ...new Set([...textsPool, ...quirkNames.map((q) => q.name), ...voiceDefs.map((v) => v.word)].filter(Boolean)),
];

/*
 * The values of the two tokens no badge source owns yet -- the reference renderer
 * computes them in fill() (吧唧渲染参考.html) and badge_ui.c will fill
 * badge_ui_vars_t.tt_xy / tt_text. They are the prototype's own placeholder wording,
 * not strings this firmware ships, so a miss here is informational; it is reported
 * against the face of the elements that use the token so that adopting the wording is
 * a decision somebody makes rather than a discovery on the glass.
 */
const REFERENCE_RUNTIME_TOKENS = {
  ttXY: {
    values: ['点一个靶点'],
    cite: "吧唧渲染参考.html fill(): ttXY = ST.tt.xy ? `坐标 x ${x}, y ${y}` : '点一个靶点'",
  },
  ttText: {
    values: ['5 个都对，方向正确', '已点中 0 / 5'],
    cite: "吧唧渲染参考.html fill(): ttText = hit>=5 ? '5 个都对，方向正确' : `已点中 ${n} / 5`",
  },
};

const TOKEN_VALUES = {
  time: [],
  bat: [],
  coins: [],
  growth: [],
  nextGrowth: [],
  step: [],
  n: [],
  ver: [],
  pairCode: [],
  food: [],
  mood: [],
  clean: [],
  energy: [],
  health: [],
  stage: stageNames,
  name: namePool,
  peer: namePool,
  peerBreed: namePool,
  phoneText: ['phoneOn', 'phoneOff'].map(txt),
  syncText: ['syncWait', 'synced'].map(txt),
  brightText: ['低', '中', '高'],
  voiceState: ['listening', 'thinking'].map(txt),
  meetGift: [txt('meetGift')],
  sleepNow: [txt('sleepNow')],
  ttXY: [],
  ttText: [],
  placeIcon: [],
  guideIcon: [],
  placeText: placeDefs.map((p) => p.text),
  placeSub: placeDefs.map((p) => p.sub),
  guideTitle: guideDefs.map((g) => g.title),
  guideSub: guideDefs.map((g) => g.sub),
};
for (const k of Object.keys(TOKEN_VALUES)) {
  if (TOKEN_VALUES[k].some((v) => v === null || v === undefined)) {
    bail(`the value of {${k}} could not be resolved from ${SPEC_DATA_C}/${SPEC_DATA_H}`);
  }
}

const careLabelValues = (key) => {
  if (!careKeys.includes(key)) return null;
  const def = careDefs.find((c) => c.key === key);
  return [def.label, ...blocksOfCare(key).map((b) => b.text)];
};

/**
 * Every code point reachable in a template once the runtime has expanded it, with
 * the chain of values that got there (for the failure message).
 *
 * `seen` guards against a value that contains its own token; badge_ui_expand() caps
 * re-expansion at four levels, and so does this.
 */
function reachable(tmpl, seen, depth = 0) {
  for (const ch of tmpl) seen.add(ch.codePointAt(0));
  if (depth >= 4) return seen;
  for (const m of tmpl.matchAll(/\{([^{}]*)\}/g)) {
    const token = m[1];
    let values;
    if (token.startsWith('careLabel:')) {
      values = careLabelValues(token.slice('careLabel:'.length));
      if (!values) {
        bail(
          `"${clip(tmpl)}" uses {${token}}, whose key is not one of badge_care_keys[] ` +
            `(${careKeys.join(', ')}) -- the runtime would draw it literally`,
        );
      }
    } else if (token in TOKEN_VALUES) {
      values = TOKEN_VALUES[token];
    } else {
      bail(
        `"${clip(tmpl)}" uses {${token}}, which is not a token this check knows.\n` +
          '  An unmodelled token means the check cannot prove that string is drawable,\n' +
          '  so it stops instead of reporting a number it cannot stand behind. Add the\n' +
          '  token to TOKEN_VALUES (and to tests/host/test_badge_geom.c if the runtime\n' +
          '  grew it) rather than letting this pass.',
      );
    }
    for (const v of values) reachable(v, seen, depth + 1);
  }
  return seen;
}

/* ============================================================== the report ====*/

const describe = (cp, where, text) => `  ${u(cp)}  ${esc(cp)}  '${String.fromCodePoint(cp)}'  in ${where}: "${clip(text)}"`;

const reportMisses = (label, misses, { hard }) => {
  const shown = LIST_ALL ? misses : misses.slice(0, MAX_LISTED);
  console.log(`${hard ? 'MISSING' : 'informational'} ${label}: ${misses.length} code point(s)`);
  for (const m of shown) for (const line of m.lines) console.log(line);
  if (misses.length > shown.length) {
    console.log(`  ... and ${misses.length - shown.length} more (--all lists every one)`);
  }
};

console.log('check-font-coverage - can the badge draw every character it ships?');
console.log(`  spec    ${SPEC_PATH}`);
console.log(`  tables  main/ui/badge_spec_data.c (generated by tools/gen-badge-ui.mjs)`);
console.log(`  fonts   ${FONTS_DIR}`);
console.log(`  mode    ${STRICT ? '--strict: informational misses fail too' : 'informational misses are reported, not fatal'}`);

if (!existsSync(SPEC_PATH)) {
  bail(
    `spec not found at ${SPEC_PATH}\n` +
      '  Extract the interaction specification to _ref/baji-spec/ or pass --spec <path>.',
  );
}

/* --- the faces ----------------------------------------------------------------*/

const specJson = JSON.parse(readFileSync(SPEC_PATH, 'utf8'));
const specFonts = specJson.fonts ?? {};

title('faces');
for (const f of faces.values()) {
  const key = f.name.replace(/^badge_/, '');
  const declared = specFonts[key]?.chars;
  console.log(
    `  ${f.name}.c  ${String(f.covered.size).padStart(5)} code points in ${f.segments} cmap segments` +
      (declared ? `   (badge_spec.json fonts.${key}.chars = "${declared}")` : '   (not in badge_spec.json fonts)'),
  );
}
console.log(`  union of the faces: ${allFaces.size} code points`);

/* --- 1. per-element coverage --------------------------------------------------*/

const screenDefs = (() => {
  const b = tableBody('badge_screens');
  return braceRows(b.body, b.start).map((r) => {
    const f = rowFields(r.text);
    return { first: Number(f[4]), count: Number(f[5]) };
  });
})();
const screenOf = (elIndex) => {
  const i = screenDefs.findIndex((s) => elIndex >= s.first && elIndex < s.first + s.count);
  return i < 0 ? '?' : `screen ${i}`;
};

title('[1/3] per-element coverage - every code point an element can draw, against ITS OWN face');
console.log(`      ${elements.length} elements from badge_els[], templates and {token} values from the generated tables`);

const perFace = new Map([...faces.keys()].map((n) => [n, { els: 0, cps: new Set(), misses: new Map() }]));
const tokenNames = new Set();
const templates = new Set();

for (const el of elements) {
  const face = faceNameOf(el.font);
  if (!face || !faces.has(face)) {
    bail(`badge_els[${el.index}] (${SPEC_DATA_C}:${el.line}) names face "${el.font}", which is not one of ${[...faces.keys()].join(', ')}`);
  }
  const bucket = perFace.get(face);
  bucket.els++;
  const tmpl = strAt(el.textIdx);
  templates.add(tmpl);
  for (const m of tmpl.matchAll(/\{([^{}]*)\}/g)) tokenNames.add(m[1]);
  const need = reachable(tmpl, new Set());
  for (const cp of need) {
    bucket.cps.add(cp);
    if (cp < 0x80) continue;
    if (faces.get(face).covered.has(cp)) continue;
    if (!bucket.misses.has(cp)) bucket.misses.set(cp, []);
    bucket.misses.get(cp).push(
      describe(
        cp,
        `badge_els[${el.index}] '${el.id}' (${screenOf(el.index)}, ${face})`,
        tmpl,
      ),
    );
  }
}

let elMisses = 0;
for (const [name, b] of perFace) {
  const distinct = [...b.cps].filter((cp) => cp > 0x7f).length;
  const misses = [...b.misses.entries()].sort((x, y) => x[0] - y[0]);
  elMisses += misses.length;
  console.log(
    `  ${name}: ${b.els} elements, ${distinct} distinct non-ASCII code points required -> ` +
      (misses.length ? `${misses.length} MISSING` : 'all covered'),
  );
  for (const [cp, lines] of misses.slice(0, MAX_LISTED)) {
    console.log(describe(cp, lines[0].split(' in ')[1]?.split(':')[0] ?? name, ''));
    for (const l of lines) console.log(`      ${l.trim()}`);
  }
  if (misses.length > MAX_LISTED) console.log(`  ... and ${misses.length - MAX_LISTED} more`);
}
console.log(`  ${templates.size} distinct element templates, ${tokenNames.size} distinct {tokens}: ${[...tokenNames].sort().join(' ')}`);

/*
 * 1b. The strings no element owns. The badge draws these through the composite
 * components the specification declares -- the alert bubble, the toast and the float
 * -- whose faces badge_spec_data.h names (BADGE_BUBBLE_FONT / BADGE_TOAST_FONT /
 * BADGE_FLOAT_FONT), or speaks them (a voice reply has no glyph at all, and is
 * covered here only as a bound).
 */
const componentFaces = ['BADGE_BUBBLE_FONT', 'BADGE_TOAST_FONT', 'BADGE_FLOAT_FONT']
  .map((d) => {
    const m = new RegExp(`#define\\s+${d}\\s+(BADGE_FONT_[A-Z0-9_]+)`).exec(specHdr.code);
    return m ? faceNameOf(m[1]) : null;
  })
  .filter(Boolean);
if (componentFaces.length === 0) bail(`${SPEC_DATA_H}: no BADGE_BUBBLE_FONT/BADGE_TOAST_FONT/BADGE_FLOAT_FONT`);
const componentCovered = (cp) => componentFaces.some((f) => faces.get(f)?.covered.has(cp));

const componentStrings = [
  ...textsPool.map((t) => ({ where: 'badge_strings[] texts pool (BADGE_TXT_*)', text: t })),
  ...alertDefs.map((a, i) => ({ where: `badge_alerts[${i}].text (${SPEC_DATA_C}:${a.line})`, text: a.text })),
  ...careBlocks.map((b) => ({ where: `badge_blocks[${b.index}].text (${SPEC_DATA_C}:${b.line})`, text: b.text })),
  ...voiceDefs.flatMap((v, i) => [
    { where: `badge_voice_defs[${i}].word (spoken)`, text: v.word },
    { where: `badge_voice_defs[${i}].reply (spoken)`, text: v.reply },
  ]),
  ...touchTargets.map((t, i) => ({ where: `badge_touch_targets[${i}].label`, text: t.label })),
].filter((s) => s.text);

const sharedMisses = new Map();
for (const { where, text } of componentStrings) {
  for (const ch of nonAscii(text)) {
    const cp = ch.codePointAt(0);
    if (componentCovered(cp)) continue;
    if (!sharedMisses.has(cp)) sharedMisses.set(cp, []);
    sharedMisses.get(cp).push(describe(cp, where, text));
  }
}
const sharedSorted = [...sharedMisses.entries()].sort((a, b) => a[0] - b[0]);
console.log(
  `  component vocabulary (${componentStrings.length} strings drawn by the bubble/toast/float ` +
    `faces: ${componentFaces.join(', ')}): ` +
    (sharedSorted.length ? `${sharedSorted.length} MISSING` : 'all covered'),
);
for (const [cp, lines] of sharedSorted.slice(0, MAX_LISTED)) {
  console.log(lines[0]);
  for (const l of lines.slice(1)) console.log(`      ${l.trim()}`);
}
if (sharedSorted.length > MAX_LISTED) console.log(`  ... and ${sharedSorted.length - MAX_LISTED} more`);
const hard1 = elMisses + sharedSorted.length;

/*
 * 1c. The two tokens no firmware source owns yet (see REFERENCE_RUNTIME_TOKENS).
 * Measured against the face of each element that uses the token, so a miss names the
 * face that would show the blank box -- but informational, because the wording is the
 * prototype's placeholder and adopting it is a decision badge_ui.c has not made.
 */
const referenceTokenMisses = new Map();
for (const [token, info] of Object.entries(REFERENCE_RUNTIME_TOKENS)) {
  for (const el of elements) {
    const tmpl = strAt(el.textIdx);
    if (!tmpl.includes(`{${token}}`)) continue;
    const face = faceNameOf(el.font);
    for (const value of info.values) {
      for (const ch of nonAscii(value)) {
        const cp = ch.codePointAt(0);
        if (faces.get(face).covered.has(cp)) continue;
        if (!referenceTokenMisses.has(cp)) referenceTokenMisses.set(cp, []);
        referenceTokenMisses.get(cp).push(
          describe(
            cp,
            `badge_els[${el.index}] '${el.id}' (${screenOf(el.index)}, ${face}) if {${token}} uses the reference's wording`,
            value,
          ),
        );
      }
    }
  }
}
const referenceTokenList = [...referenceTokenMisses.entries()]
  .sort((a, b) => a[0] - b[0])
  .map(([cp, lines]) => ({ cp, lines }));
if (referenceTokenList.length) {
  console.log(
    `  {ttXY}/{ttText} as the reference renderer words them: ` +
      `${referenceTokenList.length} code point(s) the face of the element that uses the token cannot draw`,
  );
  for (const m of (LIST_ALL ? referenceTokenList : referenceTokenList.slice(0, MAX_LISTED))) {
    console.log(m.lines[0]);
    for (const l of m.lines.slice(1, 3)) console.log(`      ${l.trim()}`);
  }
  console.log(
    '      (informational: the package ships no wording for these two tokens, so the\n' +
      '       firmware supplies its own. badge_ui.c now owns them, and the words it uses\n' +
      '       are checked by class 2 below; changing them to the reference\'s wording would\n' +
      '       need the face named above to draw the reference\'s code points.)',
  );
}

/* --- 2. firmware literals that reach the glass --------------------------------*/

title('[2/3] firmware literals that reach the glass - each must exist in at least one face');

/*
 * Sources whose non-ASCII literals are text the badge puts on the glass. A file that
 * does not exist is skipped silently (each is asserted to exist elsewhere in the suite),
 * and this list is written out rather than derived so that adding a glass-facing source
 * is a deliberate edit.
 */
const GLASS_SOURCES = [
  'main/ui/badge_paint.c', // the face canvas: disc, arcs, icons, ring arrows
  'main/ui/badge_paint_priv.h',
  'main/ui/badge_geom.c', // the box arithmetic and wrapping the labels go through
  'main/ui/badge_geom.h',
  'main/ui/badge_cat.c', // the 3D cat layer
  'main/ui/cat_view.c',
  'main/ui/badge_fsm.c', // toasts, bubbles, the refusal wording
  'main/ui/badge_fsm.h',
  'main/ui/badge_ui.h',
  'main/ui/badge_ui.c', // the screen system
  'main/ui/badge_ui_plan.c',
  'main/ui/badge_ui_plan.h',
  'main/ui/badge_assets.c',
  'main/main.c', // the application tick
  'main/net/app_link.c', // the encounter prompts ("遇见", "成为朋友")
  'main/hw/badge_hw.c',
  'main/pet/badge_pet.c',
  'main/pet/badge_pet.h',
  'main/net/badge_events.h',
];

/*
 * Sources whose non-ASCII is text for something other than this glass. This list is
 * the reason the check above is trustworthy: without it, "the App's handshake
 * wording has no glyph here" would be reported as a badge defect and the check would
 * be narrowed until it was silent.
 *
 *   main/net/net_proto.c   builds the social handshake text the PHONE renders
 *   main/net/net_social.c  the peer-summary frame the App decodes
 *   main/pet/pet_state.c   the item, outfit, breed and goal names the App shows
 *   main/net/net_link.c    no text
 *   main/ui/badge_assets.h, main/ui/badge_spec.h, main/ui/badge_spec_data.h,
 *   main/ui/badge_spec_data.c
 *                          comments and generated ASCII escapes only (checked here,
 *                          reported, never fatal)
 */
const NOT_GLASS_SOURCES = [
  'main/net/net_proto.c',
  'main/net/net_social.c',
  'main/pet/pet_state.c',
  'main/net/net_link.c',
  'main/ui/badge_assets.h',
  'main/ui/badge_spec.h',
  'main/ui/badge_spec_data.h',
  'main/ui/badge_spec_data.c',
];

const readSource = (rel) => {
  const p = join(ROOT, rel);
  if (!existsSync(p)) return null;
  return { rel, src: readFileSync(p, 'utf8') };
};

const literalsOf = (rel, src) => {
  const { strings: lits } = lexC(src);
  return lits.map((l) => ({ where: `${rel}:${l.line}`, text: unescapeC(l.body) }));
};

const glassScanned = [];
const glassMissing = new Map();
for (const rel of GLASS_SOURCES) {
  const f = readSource(rel);
  if (!f) continue;
  glassScanned.push(rel);
  for (const { where, text } of literalsOf(f.rel, f.src)) {
    for (const ch of nonAscii(text)) {
      const cp = ch.codePointAt(0);
      if (rel === 'main/ui/badge_ui.c' || rel === 'main/ui/badge_fsm.c') {
        for (const name of ['badge_f16', 'badge_f20', 'badge_f24']) {
          if (!faces.get(name).covered.has(cp)) {
            hardMisses++;
            console.error(`dynamic UI ${where}: ${name} lacks ${u(cp)} ${ch}`);
          }
        }
      }
      if (allFaces.has(cp)) continue;
      if (!glassMissing.has(cp)) glassMissing.set(cp, []);
      glassMissing.get(cp).push(describe(cp, where, text));
    }
  }
}
const glassSorted = [...glassMissing.entries()].sort((a, b) => a[0] - b[0]);
console.log(`  ${glassScanned.length} sources scanned (${GLASS_SOURCES.length} in the class, ${GLASS_SOURCES.length - glassScanned.length} not written yet)`);
console.log(
  `  non-ASCII literals all covered by at least one face: ` +
    (glassSorted.length ? `NO -- ${glassSorted.length} code point(s) missing` : 'yes'),
);
for (const [, lines] of glassSorted.slice(0, MAX_LISTED)) {
  console.log(lines[0]);
  for (const l of lines.slice(1)) console.log(`      ${l.trim()}`);
}
if (glassSorted.length > MAX_LISTED) console.log(`  ... and ${glassSorted.length - MAX_LISTED} more`);

/* --- 3. the symbol list itself ------------------------------------------------*/

title('[3/3] the specification symbol list - chars_ui.txt against the faces that declare it');
if (!existsSync(CHARS_PATH)) bail(`symbol list not found at ${CHARS_PATH}`);
const charsUi = new Set([...readFileSync(CHARS_PATH, 'utf8')].map((c) => c.codePointAt(0)));
if (charsUi.size === 0) bail(`${CHARS_PATH} is empty`);

const uiFaces = [...faces.values()].filter(
  (f) => specFonts[f.name.replace(/^badge_/, '')]?.chars === 'ui',
);
if (uiFaces.length === 0) {
  bail(
    'no face declares badge_spec.json fonts.*.chars == "ui", so there is nothing to ' +
      'hold charset_ui.txt to',
  );
}
console.log(`  ${CHARS_PATH.replace(ROOT + '\\', '').replace(ROOT + '/', '')}: ${charsUi.size} code points`);
const symbolMisses = [];
for (const f of faces.values()) {
  const missing = [...charsUi].filter((cp) => !f.covered.has(cp)).sort((a, b) => a - b);
  const declares = specFonts[f.name.replace(/^badge_/, '')]?.chars;
  const required = uiFaces.includes(f);
  console.log(
    `  ${f.name}: ${charsUi.size - missing.length}/${charsUi.size} of the symbol list` +
      `   (declares "${declares}"${required ? ', held to it' : ', not a UI face'})`,
  );
  if (required) {
    for (const cp of missing) {
      symbolMisses.push(
        `${u(cp)}  ${esc(cp)}  '${String.fromCodePoint(cp)}'  in ${CHARS_PATH.replace(/\\/g, '/')}` +
          `, not in ${f.name}.c (which declares chars = "ui")`,
      );
    }
  }
}
if (symbolMisses.length === 0) console.log('  every UI face covers the whole symbol list');

const hard3 = symbolMisses.length;

/* --- the informational classes -------------------------------------------------*/

title('informational - reported and counted, NOT fatal (--strict makes them fatal)');

const jsonStrings = (() => {
  const out = [];
  const walk = (v, path) => {
    if (typeof v === 'string') {
      if (v.length) out.push({ path, text: v });
    } else if (Array.isArray(v)) {
      v.forEach((x, i) => walk(x, `${path}[${i}]`));
    } else if (v && typeof v === 'object') {
      for (const [k, x] of Object.entries(v)) walk(x, `${path}.${k}`);
    }
  };
  walk(specJson, '');
  return out;
})();

/* The JSON paths whose strings class 1 already measures. Everything else is prose. */
const RENDERED_PATH = [
  /^\.screens\.[^.]+\.els\[\d+\]\.text$/,
  /^\.texts\./,
  /^\.care\.[^.]+\.label$/,
  /^\.care\.[^.]+\.block\[\d+\]\[3\]$/,
  /^\.alerts\[\d+\]\[2\]$/,
  /^\.places\.[^.]+\.(text|sub)$/,
  /^\.guide\[\d+\]\[[12]\]$/,
  /^\.quirks\[\d+\]\.name$/,
  /^\.voice\[\d+\]\[[13]\]$/,
  /^\.stats\[\d+\]\[1\]$/,
  /^\.stages\[\d+\]\[1\]$/,
  /^\.touchTest\[\d+\]\[1\]$/,
];
const EVENT_PATH = /^\.events\[\d+\]\[[34]\]$/;

const proseStrings = jsonStrings.filter(
  (s) => !RENDERED_PATH.some((re) => re.test(s.path)) && !EVENT_PATH.test(s.path),
);
const eventStrings = jsonStrings.filter((s) => EVENT_PATH.test(s.path));

const bucket = (stringsWithPaths, where) => {
  const misses = new Map();
  for (const { path, text } of stringsWithPaths) {
    for (const ch of nonAscii(text)) {
      const cp = ch.codePointAt(0);
      if (allFaces.has(cp)) continue;
      if (!misses.has(cp)) misses.set(cp, []);
      misses.get(cp).push(describe(cp, `badge_spec.json ${path}`, text));
    }
  }
  const list = [...misses.entries()].sort((a, b) => a[0] - b[0]).map(([cp, lines]) => ({ cp, lines }));
  console.log(
    `  ${where}: ${stringsWithPaths.length} strings, ${list.length} distinct non-ASCII code point(s) with no glyph`,
  );
  for (const m of (LIST_ALL ? list : list.slice(0, MAX_LISTED))) console.log(m.lines[0]);
  if (list.length > MAX_LISTED && !LIST_ALL) {
    console.log(`  ... and ${list.length - MAX_LISTED} more (--all lists every one)`);
  }
  return list;
};

const proseMisses = bucket(proseStrings, 'spec prose (badge_spec.json .doc/.hw.rows/.fontFile/... -- never drawn)');
const eventMisses = bucket(eventStrings, 'the 14 uplink-event descriptions (events[i][3], [4]: the App decoder\'s numbers)');

const notGlassMissing = new Map();
const notGlassScanned = [];
for (const rel of NOT_GLASS_SOURCES) {
  const f = readSource(rel);
  if (!f) continue;
  notGlassScanned.push(rel);
  for (const { where, text } of literalsOf(f.rel, f.src)) {
    for (const ch of nonAscii(text)) {
      const cp = ch.codePointAt(0);
      if (allFaces.has(cp)) continue;
      if (!notGlassMissing.has(cp)) notGlassMissing.set(cp, []);
      notGlassMissing.get(cp).push(describe(cp, `${where} (not on the glass)`, text));
    }
  }
}
const notGlassList = [...notGlassMissing.entries()].sort((a, b) => a[0] - b[0]).map(([cp, lines]) => ({ cp, lines }));
console.log(
  `  App/radio-facing and legacy sources (${notGlassScanned.length} scanned): ` +
    `${notGlassList.length} distinct non-ASCII code point(s) with no glyph`,
);
for (const m of (LIST_ALL ? notGlassList : notGlassList.slice(0, MAX_LISTED))) {
  console.log(m.lines[0]);
}
if (notGlassList.length > MAX_LISTED && !LIST_ALL) {
  console.log(`  ... and ${notGlassList.length - MAX_LISTED} more (--all lists every one)`);
}

const soft = proseMisses.length + eventMisses.length + notGlassList.length + referenceTokenList.length;
console.log(
  `\n  ${soft} informational code point(s): a clean run does NOT mean every character in the\n` +
    '  package has a glyph -- it means every character the badge DRAWS does. The three\n' +
    '  groups above are the difference, with their origin on each line.',
);
if (STRICT) {
  console.log(
    '  --strict was passed, so they are failures here. --strict is expected to fail\n' +
      '  today: the faces are subsets of Noto Sans SC built from the specification\'s own\n' +
      '  chars_ui.txt, which by construction does not contain the prose, the App-facing\n' +
      '  text or the event-description table.',
  );
}

/* --- verdict -------------------------------------------------------------------*/

const hard = hard1 + glassSorted.length + hard3;
hardMisses = hard;
softMisses = soft;

title('result');
console.log(`  class 1  per-element + component vocabulary : ${hard1 ? `${hard1} MISSING` : 'ok'}`);
console.log(`  class 2  firmware literals on the glass     : ${glassSorted.length ? `${glassSorted.length} MISSING` : 'ok'}`);
console.log(`  class 3  the specification symbol list      : ${hard3 ? `${hard3} MISSING` : 'ok'}`);
console.log(`  informational (${STRICT ? 'promoted by --strict' : 'not fatal'})          : ${soft}`);

if (hard === 0 && (!STRICT || soft === 0)) {
  console.log(
    '\nOK: every character the badge draws or says has a glyph in the face that draws it.\n' +
      'The fonts are subsets, so a character that is NOT in the subset is a blank box on the\n' +
      'device: do not narrow this check, regenerate the face instead (see the licence file\n' +
      'in main/ui/fonts/ for the lv_font_conv command).',
  );
  process.exit(0);
}

console.log(
  '\nFAIL: the badge would draw a blank box.\n' +
    'Fix: the fonts are subsets, so the missing characters have to be added to the\n' +
    'lv_font_conv symbol list and the affected face regenerated (main/ui/fonts/, see the\n' +
    'licence file there for the command). Do not narrow this check: a character that is\n' +
    'not in the subset is a blank box on the device.',
);
process.exit(1);
